course: money
lesson: 9
title: The piggy bank and a jar for each goal
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: A piggy bank that is hard to open helps you save, because getting money out takes effort, so you do not spend it on a whim.
explain: Ella uses three jars, SPEND, SAVE and GIVE, and splits her £6 into £2 for each: one jar for now, one for later, one to share; sharing money out like this is called allocating.
explain: A separate jar for each goal stops one goal eating another: Zara puts £5 a week in her bike jar and £2 in her jar for Mum's present, so she can see both goals growing.
explain: "Pay yourself first" means you put your savings aside before you spend anything, so you cannot spend them by mistake, and a safe place keeps them from little brothers.
fact: A piggy bank or money box is a container for saving coins.
fact: A piggy bank that is hard to open makes it harder to spend savings on a whim.
fact: Many families use three jars: spend (for now), save (for later) and give (to share).
fact: To allocate money is to share it out for different uses.
fact: A separate jar for each goal lets you see each goal clearly and stops one goal using up another.
fact: Moving money out of savings is allowed, but it takes you further from your goal, so stop and think first.
fact: A charity is a group that helps people or animals; to donate is to give money to help.
fact: Long-term saving is for things far away in time; short-term saving is for things soon.
fact: Seeing your coins pile up in a see-through jar helps keep you going; this push to keep going is called motivation.
fact: Money boxes with counters can be wrong, so count yourself now and then.
fact: "Pay yourself first" means putting money into savings before spending any.
fact: Money needs a safe place, such as a locked box or a bank, so it is not lost or taken.
exercise: Ella splits £6 equally into SPEND, SAVE and GIVE jars. How much goes in each? | £2 | £3 | £6 | £1.50
exercise: Why does a piggy bank with no easy opening help Jamie save? | Getting money out takes effort, so he doesn't spend on a whim | It makes the coins worth more | It hides the money from the bank | It stops the coins rusting
exercise: Jamie puts £1 a week into his piggy bank for 30 weeks. How much is inside? | £30 | £13 | £31 | £3
exercise: Zara puts £2 a week into her jar for Mum's £10 present. How many weeks to fill it? | 5 | 4 | 8 | 10
exercise: Zara also puts £5 a week in her bike jar. How much is in it after 5 weeks? | £25 | £10 | £35 | £30
exercise: Cian had £12 in his sock drawer. His little brother spent £4. How much is left? | £8 | £16 | £4 | £6
exercise: What is a safer place for savings than a sock drawer? | A locked money box or a bank | Under the doormat by the front door | In a coat pocket hanging in the hall | Behind the TV where nobody looks
exercise: Noah moves £5 from his £15 save jar to his £3 spend jar. How much is in each now? | Save £10, spend £8 | Save £15, spend £8 | Save £10, spend £3 | Save £20, spend £8
exercise: Priya puts 50p a week into her give jar for 8 weeks. How much is there? | £4 | £8 | £2 | £4.50
exercise: What does it mean to donate to a charity? | To give money to a group that helps people or animals | To borrow money from a group | To save money in a bank | To pay for a toy in a shop
exercise: Lars puts £1 a week into his "one day" jar. How much after 2 years (104 weeks)? | £104 | £52 | £24 | £100
exercise: Ivy adds 3 coins a week to her see-through jar. How many coins after 10 weeks? | 30 | 13 | 10 | 33
exercise: Oscar's money box shows £18.40. He adds a 50p. What should it show now? | £18.90 | £18.45 | £23.40 | £19.40
exercise: Erin gets £5 and "pays herself first" by saving £1. How much can she spend? | £4 | £5 | £6 | £1
exercise: What does "pay yourself first" mean? | Put money into savings before spending any | Buy yourself a treat before anything else | Ask to be paid before doing a job | Pay back what you owe straight away
exercise: What is long-term saving? | Saving for something far away in time | Saving for something you'll buy tomorrow | Spending money slowly over a long time | Saving only on the longest days of the year
test: Cian yells "ya wee rat!" ("wee" means little) after his brother spends £4 of his £12. What's the lesson for next time? | Keep savings somewhere safe, like a locked box | Spend everything before anyone else can | Hide money in a different sock | Never save at all
test: Noah's mum asks "you sure?" before he moves £5 from save to spend. Why does she ask? | Moving it puts his saving goal further away | Moving money between jars is against the law | The £5 will simply disappear on the way across | Spend jars can never hold more than £3 at a time
test: Zara's goal: £60 bike. After 5 weeks her bike jar has £25 and Mum's jar is full, so now all £7 a week goes to the bike. How many more weeks? | 5 | 4 | 7 | 6
test: Ivy says her see-through jar is "satisfying, innit". What's the idea behind it? | Seeing progress keeps you motivated to save | Glass jars make the money inside grow faster | Coins kept in glass jars are worth more than others | It's easier for brothers and sisters to borrow from
test: Oscar's counter says £18.40, but when he counts the coins he gets £17.40. What should he trust? | His own careful count, because counters can be wrong | The counter, because machines are never wrong | Neither, money can't be counted | Add both and say £35.80
test: Erin's like "pay MYSELF? I'm the one getting paid, that's delulu." ("Delulu" means deluded.) What does Dad really mean? | Put your savings aside first, before you spend | Hand your money to yourself across a shop counter | Pay Dad first as a thank-you for the good advice | Spend it all on yourself first, then save the rest
test: A Welsh nan says "lush, cariad, a jar for giving!" ("lush" means lovely; "cariad" means love.) What's a give jar for? | Saving to donate to people or animals who need help | Keeping money to lend at a profit | Buying sweets for yourself | Paying for school dinners
test: What would happen if Zara kept all her money in one jar for both the bike and Mum's present? | She might spend it all on one goal and forget the other | Both goals would be reached twice as fast | The money would split itself fairly | Nothing, one jar is always better
test: Lars's mate says "one day jar? that's for like, never, bro." Lars has saved £1 a week for a year. What has he got? | £52 | £12 | £365 | £104
test: Jamie never smashes his piggy bank. His sis says "it's full of air, I bet." He's put £1 in every week for 30 weeks, and £2 in one extra week. How much is inside? | £32 | £30 | £31 | £60
test: Ella gets £9 this time and splits it equally into her three jars. How much in each? | £3 | £2 | £9 | £4.50
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the principle designated "pay yourself first"? | The allocation of a sum to savings prior to any expenditure | The purchase of a reward for oneself prior to saving | The demand for remuneration prior to the performance of work | The repayment of debts prior to the receipt of income
exam: It is given that six pounds is allocated in equal portions among three receptacles. Determine the sum assigned to each. | Two pounds | Three pounds | One pound fifty pence | Six pounds exactly
exam: A sum of fifty pence is deposited weekly over a period of eight weeks. Compute the aggregate deposited. | Four pounds | Eight pounds | Two pounds | Four pounds fifty pence
exam: Identify the principal advantage of maintaining a separate receptacle in respect of each saving goal. | Each goal remains visible and is not consumed by another | The aggregate sum saved is thereby doubled | Coins so held are protected against forgery | The goals are thereby attained without saving
